WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.343 --> 00:00:02.843
(light music)

00:00:07.661 --> 00:00:10.314
- [Narrator] On the sandy
shores of the Florida Keys,

00:00:10.314 --> 00:00:13.099
sea turtles are laying their eggs

00:00:13.099 --> 00:00:17.391
but a threat is looming
for these gentle creatures.

00:00:17.391 --> 00:00:20.751
The Caribbean climate has become
increasingly unpredictable

00:00:20.751 --> 00:00:24.179
as rising sea levels cripple the beaches

00:00:24.179 --> 00:00:27.727
where these turtles annually nest.

00:00:27.727 --> 00:00:29.869
- They come back to their natal beaches

00:00:29.869 --> 00:00:31.577
so animals that were born here

00:00:31.577 --> 00:00:33.967
are coming back to this
very little spit of land

00:00:33.967 --> 00:00:36.329
in order to lay their own eggs.

00:00:36.329 --> 00:00:38.129
If the beach goes away, they may not be

00:00:38.129 --> 00:00:40.879
as flexible to use another beach.

00:00:42.393 --> 00:00:44.863
- [Narrator] Dr. Kristen Hart
and her team of researchers

00:00:44.863 --> 00:00:46.779
are working around the clock,

00:00:46.779 --> 00:00:48.516
tagging and tracking these turtles

00:00:48.516 --> 00:00:51.315
in an effort to protect their future.

00:00:51.315 --> 00:00:53.660
- They're in peril, they do need help

00:00:53.660 --> 00:00:57.827
and we're trying to figure
out where the problems are.

00:00:58.819 --> 00:01:01.319
(light music)

00:01:02.193 --> 00:01:06.332
We are here on East Key in
Dry Tortugas National Park.

00:01:06.332 --> 00:01:08.940
We are about 70 miles west of Key West

00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:11.305
and we're in the Gulf of Mexico.

00:01:11.305 --> 00:01:13.576
There are seven islands,
we're on one of the smallest

00:01:13.576 --> 00:01:16.044
but one of the densest
turtle nesting beaches.

00:01:16.044 --> 00:01:17.701
Some of them are right
on top of each other,

00:01:17.701 --> 00:01:20.684
a foot away from each other,

00:01:20.684 --> 00:01:23.417
this is an almost completely marine park

00:01:23.417 --> 00:01:27.274
and the turtles come up and
grace us with their presence.

00:01:27.274 --> 00:01:29.256
It's pretty amazing to
see these animals up close

00:01:29.256 --> 00:01:34.105
and personal with them,
and we get right in there.

00:01:34.105 --> 00:01:37.064
Generally sea turtles date back
to the time of the dinosaurs

00:01:37.064 --> 00:01:40.301
so it's 200 million years of evolution.

00:01:40.301 --> 00:01:42.395
Our study was the first
to really concentrate

00:01:42.395 --> 00:01:44.877
on the Dry Tortugas turtles.

00:01:44.877 --> 00:01:46.834
Our goals are to try to determine patterns

00:01:46.834 --> 00:01:49.765
of movement and habitat use,

00:01:49.765 --> 00:01:52.470
to figure out where they might go.

00:01:52.470 --> 00:01:54.970
(light music)

00:02:07.628 --> 00:02:11.795
All these points are either
sightings or capture points.

00:02:13.049 --> 00:02:14.225
When we first started working here,

00:02:14.225 --> 00:02:17.796
we realized that we could dip
net juvenile green turtles.

00:02:17.796 --> 00:02:19.135
I had done this in the Everglades

00:02:19.135 --> 00:02:21.319
and out here the water is much more clear,

00:02:21.319 --> 00:02:26.199
we can pursue them in the
flats and shallower areas.

00:02:26.199 --> 00:02:28.257
She's coming up.

00:02:28.257 --> 00:02:29.804
The bigger turtles out here,

00:02:29.804 --> 00:02:34.485
we do what's called a rodeo
capture, or also turtle jumping.

00:02:34.485 --> 00:02:36.337
They're usually 10 or 15 feet down

00:02:36.337 --> 00:02:37.762
but when they need to come get a breath,

00:02:37.762 --> 00:02:39.119
they're coming up to the surface

00:02:39.119 --> 00:02:41.553
and so that's when the
diver meets them down there

00:02:41.553 --> 00:02:44.066
and you basically get your
hands sort of on the rear,

00:02:44.066 --> 00:02:47.192
right behind the head, and
you get the animal pointed up.

00:02:47.192 --> 00:02:49.022
I've got him.

00:02:49.022 --> 00:02:50.540
It's a team effort,

00:02:50.540 --> 00:02:51.998
once we get them on board the boat

00:02:51.998 --> 00:02:54.120
we'll do a standard workup,
which includes measuring them,

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:57.226
weighing them, taking blood
samples, tissue samples,

00:02:57.226 --> 00:02:59.290
there's a lot of
questions that we can ask,

00:02:59.290 --> 00:03:01.498
even just about how healthy they are

00:03:01.498 --> 00:03:05.665
and how healthy then are the
resources that they're using.

00:03:08.075 --> 00:03:11.803
We also fix devices on them
to track their movement,

00:03:11.803 --> 00:03:14.898
so we've used acoustic
telemetry, radio telemetry

00:03:14.898 --> 00:03:16.892
and then satellite tracking,

00:03:16.892 --> 00:03:18.652
so we're learning a lot
about their movement paths

00:03:18.652 --> 00:03:21.069
and their areas of residence.

00:03:21.972 --> 00:03:24.472
(light music)

00:03:29.290 --> 00:03:30.735
They've been around the globe

00:03:30.735 --> 00:03:33.136
but they're hard-wired to
come back to the very place

00:03:33.136 --> 00:03:36.825
where they were born
to lay their own eggs.

00:03:36.825 --> 00:03:38.661
If an animal was born on East Key,

00:03:38.661 --> 00:03:40.086
she'll come back to East Key,

00:03:40.086 --> 00:03:41.621
she's not gonna go use Loggerhead Key

00:03:41.621 --> 00:03:43.715
or one of the other Keys here.

00:03:43.715 --> 00:03:46.215
(light music)

00:03:48.704 --> 00:03:50.193
We all take turns, every 30 minutes,

00:03:50.193 --> 00:03:51.938
walking around this beach in particular.

00:03:51.938 --> 00:03:54.239
It takes about 10 minutes to walk around.

00:03:54.239 --> 00:03:55.553
If they nest, we let them nest

00:03:55.553 --> 00:03:58.130
and mark off where the nest
is and then we work her up,

00:03:58.130 --> 00:04:01.413
just like the same workup we
would do with a little turtle.

00:04:01.413 --> 00:04:03.722
She's corralled in a box
cause they can be 400 pounds

00:04:03.722 --> 00:04:06.232
and they're very powerful

00:04:06.232 --> 00:04:08.917
and they'll do that about
every two weeks during a season.

00:04:08.917 --> 00:04:11.025
And when we get recaptures
that's really exciting,

00:04:11.025 --> 00:04:15.187
cause you know the animal still
survived, wherever she went.

00:04:15.187 --> 00:04:17.051
- [Narrator] In order for
turtles to lay their eggs,

00:04:17.051 --> 00:04:19.754
the conditions have to be just right.

00:04:19.754 --> 00:04:23.418
Ideally, they'll encounter
high tides, dark beaches

00:04:23.418 --> 00:04:26.370
and warm sea surface temperatures,

00:04:26.370 --> 00:04:29.477
otherwise they'll do
something called a false crawl

00:04:29.477 --> 00:04:31.850
in which a turtle will make
their way onto the beach

00:04:31.850 --> 00:04:33.933
only to turn back around.

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:36.523
- We don't really know
why they'll false crawl

00:04:36.523 --> 00:04:38.358
but something isn't right.

00:04:38.358 --> 00:04:39.838
Maybe they're not feeling contractions,

00:04:39.838 --> 00:04:42.122
maybe the conditions just
aren't right at that spot,

00:04:42.122 --> 00:04:44.145
maybe they dig and they
hit a piece of coral

00:04:44.145 --> 00:04:46.978
or a root, or something like that.

00:04:48.466 --> 00:04:51.614
- [Narrator] And false crawls
could become more frequent.

00:04:51.614 --> 00:04:55.336
Rising sea levels reduce the
amount of available beach space

00:04:55.336 --> 00:04:57.567
and rising temperatures
can drastically affect

00:04:57.567 --> 00:04:58.900
sand temperature.

00:04:59.944 --> 00:05:01.705
Studies show that cooler nest temperatures

00:05:01.705 --> 00:05:04.461
produce more males, while warmer nests

00:05:04.461 --> 00:05:07.132
generally produce females.

00:05:07.132 --> 00:05:08.474
- With sea level rise and climate change,

00:05:08.474 --> 00:05:09.896
they're threatened.

00:05:09.896 --> 00:05:11.024
I think we're a little more concerned

00:05:11.024 --> 00:05:13.336
about their trajectory
of their population,

00:05:13.336 --> 00:05:16.663
cause it's generally not
good for the turtle.

00:05:16.663 --> 00:05:19.163
(light music)

00:05:22.217 --> 00:05:24.378
We've learned a lot about
these Dry Tortugas turtles

00:05:24.378 --> 00:05:27.110
and it really is valuable to me to know

00:05:27.110 --> 00:05:29.306
that they value the research
that we're contributing,

00:05:29.306 --> 00:05:30.840
we're answering questions
that are important,

00:05:30.840 --> 00:05:33.056
that haven't been asked before.

00:05:33.056 --> 00:05:35.019
It's definitely demanding,
but at the same time I think

00:05:35.019 --> 00:05:37.413
it's one of the most rewarding
things we could ever do.

00:05:37.413 --> 00:05:39.784
There aren't many people allowed

00:05:39.784 --> 00:05:42.029
on this little patch of land,
so I feel very privileged

00:05:42.029 --> 00:05:45.382
to be able to play a role in
figuring these turtles out

00:05:45.382 --> 00:05:48.465
and being in this very special place.

